<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arrow Cross Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arrow Cross Party was a Hungarian fascist and antisemitic party led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-szalasi">Ferenc Szálasi</a>, which grew from his movement founded in 1935. In October 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> installed it in power after Regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a> tried to leave the war. Its militias murdered thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and it sent tens of thousands on death marches before its regime collapsed in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrow-cross-party.jpg" alt="Arrow Cross Party" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Arrow_Cross_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-szalasi">Ferenc Szálasi</a> founded the party in 1935 as the Party of National Will. It was outlawed in 1937 and reconstituted in 1939 as the Arrow Cross Party, modelled fairly explicitly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, although Szálasi often criticised the Nazi regime. Its roots lay partly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> that followed the Hungarian Soviet Republic and Red Terror of 1919 and the White Terror that answered them. Its ideology, which Szálasi called Hungarism, combined nationalism, the promotion of agriculture, anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and an antisemitism that he called a-Semitism, which held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> could not belong in European society. It also drew on Turanism and a belief in Hungarian superiority. The arrow cross emblem was taken to be a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the 1930s the party came to dominate working-class districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, defeating the Social Democrats. In the only election it contested, in May 1939, it won 15 per cent of the vote and 29 seats, though most Hungarians could not vote. Its claims to a Greater Hungary clashed with German ambitions, and Hitler delayed his endorsement for years; the Horthy government banned it at the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> occupied Hungary in March 1944, the new prime minister Döme Sztójay legalised it. In the spring and summer of that year more than 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were deported from the countryside, with the help of the Interior Ministry and the gendarmerie, both linked to the party.</p><p>In October 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a> negotiated a ceasefire with the Soviets. Germany launched Operation Panzerfaust, took him into custody and forced him to abdicate, and Szálasi became Leader of the Nation and head of a Government of National Unity on the same day. Soviet and Romanian forces were already deep in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and the government's authority shrank to a narrow band around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Arrow Cross death squads murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the streets and shot them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The party helped Adolf Eichmann restart <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and sent some 80,000 Jews out of the city. Most of Budapest's Jews were saved by a handful of Jewish leaders and diplomats, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raoul-wallenberg">Raoul Wallenberg</a>, Angelo Rotta, Carl Lutz, Ángel Sanz Briz and Giorgio Perlasca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The government fell when the Soviet Army took Pest at the end of January 1945, though Arrow Cross and German forces fought on in the west until the war ended in April. Over 6,200 murder indictments were served on Arrow Cross men within months of the war, and Szálasi was hanged in March 1946 with three of his key associates. A memorial of 2005 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> commemorates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> shot there by Arrow Cross militiamen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arrow Cross Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1944 the pro-Nazi Döme Sztójay legalised the <b>Arrow Cross Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany kidnapped his son and forced Horthy to abdicate in favour of Ferenc Szálasi, leader of the fascist <b>Arrow Cross Party</b>, who formed a Government of National Unity and took the title Leader of the Nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-national-unity">Government of National Unity</a></li></ul></section>
